<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; border: 1px solid #a7d9ed;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Helping Around the House</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> We All Do Chores.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify main ideas on familiar topics in simple paragraphs. Reading: Illustrate main ideas from readings. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify the main ideas in simple paragraphs about household chores (e.g., "Everyone helps with chores."). Can illustrate main ideas from readings about helping at home, through drawings or discussions (e.g., "I help wash the dishes."). Can identify open and closed syllables in simple words (e.g., "do" as an open syllable and "cook" as a closed syllable).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards (or pictures on a projector) of various household chores (e.g., "wash the dishes," "sweep the floor," "fold clothes," "water the plant," "make the bed"). Students call out the chore they see. The teacher then asks, "Do you do these chores at home?" and invites simple "yes" or "no" answers or short phrases like "I wash dishes."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard for "sweep the floor" and says, "I sweep the floor. /s/ sweep." Then holds up "wash the dishes" and says, "I wash the dishes. /w/ wash." The teacher writes "do" and "cook" on the board, circles "do" and says, "This is an open syllable: 'do'." Then circles 'ook' in "cook" and says, "This is a closed syllable: 'cook'." The teacher models reading short sentences using present simple like "My mom cooks every day. My dad helps clean the house."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'wash' for the floor or for clothes?" (Expected: "Clothes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'sweep' for the floor or the table?" (Expected: "Floor!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'do' an open or closed syllable?" (Expected: "Open!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Helping Around the House".</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative text/story titled "Our Chores" about a family (e.g., 'The Garcia Family') and their daily/weekly chores, presented on a worksheet or whiteboard. The text will feature sentences using present simple, frequency adverbs (every day, often, usually), and target vocabulary (wash, clean, sweep, cook, help, organize, dust, fold, feed, mop, tidy up, throw, wipe, hang, change, make, water; dishes, house, chores, broom, floor, kitchen, family, table, plant, laundry, sheets, clothes, bathroom, bedroom, living room, porch).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Text:</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"The Garcia family is very busy! Everyone helps with chores. Maria helps her mom cook dinner every day. She also sets the table. Pedro sweeps the floor in the living room. He often helps his dad wash the car. Their little sister, Sofia, helps water the plants and feeds the fish. Sometimes she tidies up her bedroom. Mom usually folds the laundry and hangs the clothes. Dad cleans the bathroom every week. They all work together to keep their house clean and organized."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension – identifying main ideas and specific details in simple paragraphs about familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> The teacher asks, "What is the story about?" (Expected: "Family chores" or "Helping at home.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> Students read statements and mark them True (T) or False (F) based on the text.</li>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Maria washes the car." (F)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Pedro sweeps the floor." (T)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Sofia waters the plants." (T)</li>
                    </ul>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Accuracy focus on Reading.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading sentences and paragraphs, identifying main ideas, using present simple tense, target vocabulary for chores, recognizing frequency adverbs, and practicing phonemic awareness (open/closed syllables, /w/ and /s/ sounds).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice – Underline the Main Idea & Circle Vocabulary:</b> Students reread the "Our Chores" text. They underline the main idea of each short paragraph (e.g., "Everyone helps with chores." or "Mom usually folds the laundry."). Then, they circle all the vocabulary words related to chores (e.g., <i>cook, dishes, sweep, water, plants, fold, laundry, cleans, bathroom</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled practice – Cloze Reading with Syllable Focus:</b> The teacher provides new, simple sentences with blanks, using the target vocabulary. Students read the sentences and fill in the missing chore verbs or nouns from a word bank. For example: "I help my mom ____ the dishes." (wash). "My sister helps to ____ the floor." (sweep). After filling in, the teacher asks students to identify if the filled word has an open or closed syllable (e.g., "wash" - closed, "sweep" - closed).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check – Reading Aloud and Sound Practice:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a short list of words (e.g., 'wash', 'sweep', 'water', 'cook', 'do', 'clean', 'fold'). Partner A reads a word; Partner B says if it has the /w/ sound (wash, water) or /s/ sound (sweep) or identifies if it's an open syllable (do) or closed syllable (cook, clean, fold). They take turns reading and identifying. They can also check if their partner correctly identified the sound/syllable.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Helping Around the House".</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Reading and Illustrating Main Ideas.</b> Students receive a worksheet with three to four short paragraphs, each describing a different family member doing their chores, using the target vocabulary and present simple tense. Each paragraph will have a blank space next to it.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students independently read each paragraph carefully.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After reading each paragraph, students identify the main idea – what chore is being done, and by whom.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In the blank space next to each paragraph, students draw a simple illustration that represents the main idea of the text (e.g., a person washing dishes, a broom sweeping the floor, a person folding clothes).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students can share their drawings with a partner and briefly explain what their picture shows based on the reading. Example: "This picture shows Ana washing the dishes."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity directly reinforces the theme "We All Do Chores" by having students comprehend and visualize different household tasks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading fluency and comprehension, visual interpretation of main ideas, and understanding of chore vocabulary in context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Formative assessment strategy.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the illustration task, focusing on student ability to identify and illustrate main ideas from the readings. A simple rubric can be used for the illustrations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' illustrated paragraphs from the Production stage will be collected. The teacher will check if the illustrations accurately reflect the main chore described in each paragraph.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of illustration: Does the drawing match the main idea of the paragraph? (e.g., if the text says "He sweeps the floor," the drawing shows a person sweeping).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identification of key vocabulary: Were target vocabulary words understood and represented?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort in task completion: All paragraphs illustrated.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What chore was easy to read about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you like drawing what you read? Why or why not?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new chore word did you read today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you read a sentence about a chore your family does?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The reading and illustration tasks engaged students well. Most students were able to identify main ideas and visually represent them, indicating good comprehension of the simple paragraphs and vocabulary. The phonemic awareness activity with open/closed syllables needs more reinforcement, perhaps with visual cues or more kinesthetic actions. Some students struggled with differentiating /w/ and /s/ when reading aloud, requiring more individual practice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking), students will use the vocabulary and present simple structures learned in this reading lesson to discuss their own household chores and ask others about their routines, focusing on oral production and interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>